<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nail B. Khelifa
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Cambridge
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">nbk24@cam.ac.uk</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Richard E. Turner
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Cambridge
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ret26@cam.ac.uk</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ramji Venkataramanan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Cambridge
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rv285@cam.ac.uk</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recursive training of generative models on their own outputs can lead to model collapse, a compounding drift away from the true data distribution. Existing theoretical works bound finite-round error accumulation in the context of diffusion models, but two questions remain open: what distribution does the recursion converge to, and how fast? We answer both, isolating a mechanism distinct from imperfect learning: even with perfect score estimation and exact sampling, the early stopping of the reverse diffusion (required for numerical stability) drives a progressive drift away from the data distribution. We prove that this recursion converges geometrically to a unique limiting distribution, which admits a closed-form characterization as an infinite mixture of increasingly Gaussian-smoothed versions of the data distribution. A Hermite spectral decomposition of this limit reveals that recursive training acts as a low-pass filter: higher-order modes, which encode fine non-Gaussian structure, are attenuated much more strongly than coarse modes. This spectral picture motivates annealed truncation schedules that progressively shrink truncation times across retraining rounds; we prove that any schedule converging to <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> asymptotically eliminates recursive compounding. Finally, we show our idealized characterization is robust: in the presence of discretization and score estimation errors, the learned distribution remains in a Wasserstein-2 ball around the ideal limit, with mode-dependent contraction rates that contract high-order errors faster than low-order ones. We validate the theory on synthetic Gaussian mixtures and CIFAR-10.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the success of generative modeling, the use of synthetic data for training has attracted considerable attention. Such generated data may be used deliberately to improve or fine-tune existing models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gulcehre et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alemohammad et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zelikman et al. (2022)</a></cite>, or may enter training sets inadvertently when data are polluted with outputs of generative models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini et al. (2024)</a></cite>. It has been found that repeatedly retraining models on their own outputs causes systematic drift away from the target distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alemohammad et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shumailov et al. (2024)</a></cite>, a phenomenon referred to as <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model collapse</span>. Subsequent work characterized collapse
as a loss of the tails of the target distribution and a decrease in the diversity of generated samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dohmatob et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2026)</a></cite>. In settings that allow for a combination of synthetic and real data in each retraining iteration, different behaviors have been observed: in some cases, a small proportion of synthetic data can significantly degrade performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dohmatob et al. (2025)</a></cite>, while in others, it has been shown that collapse can be mitigated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gerstgrasser et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dohmatob et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dey et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al. (2026)</a></cite> or completely avoided <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barzilai and Shamir (2026)</a></cite>. In some settings, synthetic data could even be beneficial when used judiciously <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dohmatob et al. (2024b)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al. (2021b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2020)</a></cite>, recent theory has quantified finite-round error propagation under population-level, finite-sample, or model-specific assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khelifa et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2025)</a></cite>. However, these works do not characterize the asymptotic behavior of iteratively retrained diffusion models, i.e., whether the model tends toward a collapsed regime in which further retraining no longer changes the learned distribution.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setting</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the true target data distribution on <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider a recursive procedure which, in each training round (generation), mixes fresh samples from <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> with synthetic samples generated by the current model. At generation <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, a proportion <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> of the training data consists of fresh samples from <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the remaining proportion <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of synthetic samples drawn from the learned model at the previous generation, denoted <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. To capture a realistic setting where training is agnostic to how samples were generated, we assume samples are not labeled as fresh or synthetic. As a result, the effective training distribution at generation <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is the mixture</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}:=\alpha\,p_{\mathrm{data}}+(1-\alpha)\,\hat{p}^{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}:=\alpha\,p_{\mathrm{data}}+(1-\alpha)\,\hat{p}^{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A score network is then trained on samples from <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the resulting diffusion model defines the next synthetic distribution <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Starting from <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{0}=p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{0}=p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, this defines the recursion</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{i}\;\xrightarrow{\text{mix with }p_{\mathrm{data}}}\;q_{i}\;\xrightarrow{\text{train model}}\;\hat{p}^{i+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0.558em">→</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">mix with </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">data</mi></msub></mrow></mover><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">train model</mtext></mover><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{i}\;\xrightarrow{\text{mix with }p_{\mathrm{data}}}\;q_{i}\;\xrightarrow{\text{train model}}\;\hat{p}^{i+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This framework matches that of existing works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khelifa et al. (2026)</a></cite>, and may be generalized to a setting where each past generation <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq i</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from in proportion <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. The goal of this work is to answer the following questions:</p>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In this recursive setting, does the sequence <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{p}^{i})_{i\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{p}^{i})_{i\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> converge to a specific distribution as <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> grows? If so, can it be characterized explicitly, and at what rate does the recursion converge to it?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Understanding this asymptotic behavior is fundamental because it identifies the regime towards which a model is driven by recursive training. Analyzing the collapsed regime highlights the role of each source of error and the mechanisms by which collapse occurs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sources of error</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training and sampling a diffusion model in this recursive setting introduce three distinct sources of error. The first, and the main focus of this paper, is <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">truncation</em>: in practice, the reverse SDE is only integrated on <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{0},T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{0},T]</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, because score estimation becomes numerically unstable as <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2022)</a></cite> (formalized in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Self-Regularization and Annealed Truncation ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>). While modern samplers introduce various corrections (e.g. Tweedie-style denoising <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Efron (2011)</a></cite>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al. (2022)</a></cite>, and other post-truncation refinements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>), the requirement of stopping the reverse SDE at <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is universal. As a result, even with perfect score estimation, exact initialization, and exact numerical sampling, the generated distribution is not <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> itself but a Gaussian-smoothed version of <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The second source of error is the score estimation error, caused by finite data and function-approximation constraints. The third is the discretization error, introduced when the reverse SDE is solved numerically. There is a rich body of work providing non-asymptotic guarantees for diffusion-based models, accounting for score estimation errors under different discretization schemes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2023c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benton et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bortoli (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2023a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conforti et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis first isolates truncation and shows that it alone induces a progressive drift away from <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> with recursive training. Errors due to imperfect score estimation and time discretization are then treated as perturbations of this idealized mechanism.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Contributions</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper develops an operator-theoretic framework for recursively trained diffusion models and characterizes their limiting behavior. Our main contributions are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2.5em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Explicit collapse distribution.</span>
We identify truncation as a primary driver of model collapse and show that, in the ideal case with no discretization or score estimation errors, the recursive dynamics (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Setting ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) converge to a unique limiting distribution <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. We derive a closed-form expression for <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> as an infinite mixture of progressively Gaussian-smoothed versions of <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and establish a geometric rate of convergence to that limit in the error-free regime (Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Collapse distribution: existence, uniqueness, and explicit form). ‣ Collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spectral structure of collapse.</span>
Decomposing the limiting distribution <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Hermite polynomial basis, we provide a spectral characterization of model collapse (Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3 (Spectral representation of the collapse distribution). ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>), which shows that recursive training acts as a low-pass filter on <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, suppressing high-order components more strongly than low-order ones, with explicit mode-dependent attenuation controlled by <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and the stopping time <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Annealed truncation schedules.</span> Having identified fixed truncation as a driver of model collapse, we introduce <em id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">annealed</em> truncation schedules with generation-adaptive truncation times <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}^{(N)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}^{(N)}</annotation></semantics></math>. We prove that any schedule converging to zero asymptotically eliminates recursive compounding in the error-free regime; schedules converging to a positive limit attenuate but do not remove compounding (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Asymptotic effect of generation-dependent truncation). ‣ Generation-dependent truncation ‣ 4 Mitigating Model Collapse with Annealed Truncation Schedules ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robustness and rate of collapse under imperfect training.</span>
We extend the analysis beyond the ideal setting to account for discretization and score estimation errors. We show that the recursive dynamics remain stable, converging at an exponential rate to a Wasserstein-2 ball around the ideal limiting distribution <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Geometric convergence and stability). ‣ Concentration around 𝑝_∞^⋆ ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 5.3 (Mode-dependent error propagation). ‣ Spectral Decomposition in the Learned Regime ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>). Thus, the limiting collapse regime becomes relevant even with a moderate number of rounds of recursive training.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Organization</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> introduces the mathematical background and the assumptions underlying our analysis. Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that, even in an idealized regime (perfect score estimation and exact sampling), model collapse occurs because of truncation of the reverse SDE as <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Mitigating Model Collapse with Annealed Truncation Schedules ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that allowing the truncation time to vary across generations changes the asymptotic behavior of the recursion. Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> provides robustness results for the idealized framework in the presence of errors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation</h5>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bold symbols (e.g. <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) denote <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued random variables or processes, and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the space of Borel probability measures on <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with finite second moment. The space of continuous functions from <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C([0,T],\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C([0,T],\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>. For random variables <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{1},\mathbf{X}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{1},\mathbf{X}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on the same probability space, we write <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{1}\perp\!\!\!\perp\mathbf{X}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mpadded style="width:0.518em;" width="0.518em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{1}\perp\!\!\!\perp\mathbf{X}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent. For a stochastic process <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{Z}_{t})_{t\in[0,T]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{Z}_{t})_{t\in[0,T]}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C([0,T],\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C([0,T],\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Law}((\mathbf{Z}_{t})_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Law</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Law}((\mathbf{Z}_{t})_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for its law on path space and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Law}(\mathbf{Z}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Law</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Law}(\mathbf{Z}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for the law of its marginal at time <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. For two measures <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\ll\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\ll\nu</annotation></semantics></math> to denote that <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is absolutely continuous with respect to <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that every <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>-null set is also <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-null. All probability measures <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> are assumed absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure on <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and, with some abuse of notation, we also use <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> to refer to their density. Calligraphic symbols (e.g. <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>) refer to operators on probability distributions i.e., mapping <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})\to\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})\to\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Id</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Id}</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity operator (<math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Id}(\mu)=\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Id</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Id}(\mu)=\mu</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>). For <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu,\nu\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu,\nu\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, the Wasserstein-2 distance between <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}(\mu,\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}(\mu,\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}(\mu,\nu)\;:=\;\left(\inf_{\gamma\in\Gamma(\mu,\nu)}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}}\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}\|^{2}\,\gamma(\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x},\mathrm{d}\mathbf{y})\right)^{1/2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐲</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}(\mu,\nu)\;:=\;\left(\inf_{\gamma\in\Gamma(\mu,\nu)}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}}\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}\|^{2}\,\gamma(\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x},\mathrm{d}\mathbf{y})\right)^{1/2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\mu,\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\mu,\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of all couplings of <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>,
i.e., probability measures on <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with marginals <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Forward diffusion</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th generation diffusion model is defined by a variance-preserving Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karatzas and Shreve (2014)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al. (2021b)</a></cite> on the interval <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>, initialized at the mixture <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\mathbf{X}_{t}^{i}=-\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{X}_{t}^{i}\,\mathrm{d}t+\mathrm{d}\mathbf{B}_{t},\qquad\mathbf{X}_{0}^{i}\sim q_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\mathbf{X}_{t}^{i}=-\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{X}_{t}^{i}\,\mathrm{d}t+\mathrm{d}\mathbf{B}_{t},\qquad\mathbf{X}_{0}^{i}\sim q_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{B}_{t})_{t\in[0,T]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{B}_{t})_{t\in[0,T]}</annotation></semantics></math> is a standard <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Brownian motion. For each time <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>, the OU process (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Forward diffusion ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) defines a sampling operator <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{t}:\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})\to\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{t}:\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})\to\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ethier and Kurtz (1986)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Øksendal (2014)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bakry et al. (2014)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Da Prato and Zabczyk (2014)</a></cite> that propagates the initial distribution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> through the diffusion as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{t}q_{i}:=\mathrm{Law}(e^{-t/2}\mathbf{X}_{0}+\sqrt{1-e^{-t}}\mathbf{Z}),\quad\mathbf{X}_{0}\sim q_{i}\perp\!\!\!\perp\mathbf{Z}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{d})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>Law</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.240em;" width="0.240em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{t}q_{i}:=\mathrm{Law}(e^{-t/2}\mathbf{X}_{0}+\sqrt{1-e^{-t}}\mathbf{Z}),\quad\mathbf{X}_{0}\sim q_{i}\perp\!\!\!\perp\mathbf{Z}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{d}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we denote the time-<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> marginal of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Forward diffusion ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i,t}:=\mathrm{Law}(\mathbf{X}_{t}^{i})=\mathcal{U}_{t}q_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Law</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i,t}:=\mathrm{Law}(\mathbf{X}_{t}^{i})=\mathcal{U}_{t}q_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The sampling operator <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is central to our analysis, and we provide extended background on its properties in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Extended Background and Preliminary Results ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reverse-time generation and truncation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under standard regularity conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anderson (1982)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haussmann and Pardoux (1986)</a></cite>, the time reversal of (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Forward diffusion ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) solves the reverse-time SDE integrated backward from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=T</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\mathbf{Y}_{s}^{i,\star}=\big[-\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{Y}_{s}^{i,\star}-\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log q_{i,s}(\mathbf{Y}_{s}^{i,\star})\big]\,\mathrm{d}s+\mathrm{d}\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{s}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋆</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋆</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋆</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\mathbf{Y}_{s}^{i,\star}=\big[-\tfrac{1}{2}\mathbf{Y}_{s}^{i,\star}-\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log q_{i,s}(\mathbf{Y}_{s}^{i,\star})\big]\,\mathrm{d}s+\mathrm{d}\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{s})_{s\in[0,T]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{s})_{s\in[0,T]}</annotation></semantics></math> is another Brownian motion on the same probability space. If we run the reverse-time SDE starting from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}_{T}^{i,\star}\sim q_{i,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐘</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋆</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}_{T}^{i,\star}\sim q_{i,T}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}_{0}^{i,\star}\sim q_{i,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐘</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋆</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}_{0}^{i,\star}\sim q_{i,0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anderson (1982)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haussmann and Pardoux (1986)</a></cite>. The true scores <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log q_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log q_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> are unknown and are approximated by a learned time-dependent network <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> trained on samples from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Setting ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). However, since <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> may be non-smooth or singular, the score <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log q_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log q_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> can become unstable as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2023b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>. Therefore, as is common in the literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khelifa et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benton et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2023c)</a></cite>, we work on a truncated interval <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{0},T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{0},T]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, where the diffusion has already regularized the law: under suitable nondegeneracy and regularity assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aronson (1967)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aronson (1968)</a></cite>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a smooth density for every <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the score is well defined and better behaved away from the singular endpoint.
Substituting the learned score <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\theta_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\theta_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> into (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Reverse-time generation and truncation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) yields</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{s}^{i}=\big[-\tfrac{1}{2}\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{s}^{i}-\mathbf{s}_{\theta_{i}}(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{s}^{i},s)\big]\mathrm{d}s+\mathrm{d}\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{s}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{s}^{i}=\big[-\tfrac{1}{2}\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{s}^{i}-\mathbf{s}_{\theta_{i}}(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{s}^{i},s)\big]\mathrm{d}s+\mathrm{d}\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with initialization <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{T}^{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{T}^{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The next generation model is therefore <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{i+1}:=\mathrm{Law}(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{t_{0}}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Law</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{i+1}:=\mathrm{Law}(\hat{\mathbf{Y}}_{t_{0}}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sampling operators</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reverse SDEs (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Reverse-time generation and truncation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Reverse-time generation and truncation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) define two sampling procedures. In the absence of score estimation and discretization errors, the reverse sampling operator for (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Reverse-time generation and truncation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is exactly <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{i+1}=\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{i+1}=\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Accounting for these two errors, the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">learned sampling operator</span> at generation <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{S}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{S}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{S}}_{i}(q_{i})=\hat{p}^{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{S}}_{i}(q_{i})=\hat{p}^{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. These operators formally define the one-step recursion in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Setting ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Regularity conditions</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{reg}}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{reg}}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of probability distributions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following assumptions:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A1)</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> admits a strictly positive smooth density with respect to the Lebesgue measure:
<math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\mathbf{x})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\mathbf{x})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A2)</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finite second moment:
<math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\|\mathbf{x}\|^{2}\mu(\mathbf{x})\,\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}\|\mathbf{x}\|^{2}\mu(\mathbf{x})\,\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S2.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A3)</span></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The score <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log\mu(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log\mu(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is
square-integrable under <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Throughout, we assume that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}\in\mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{reg}}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>reg</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}\in\mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{reg}}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, and observe that assumptions <a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="Item A1 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>–<a href="#S2.I1.i3" title="Item A3 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a> are preserved under the forward diffusion (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Forward diffusion ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and under convex combinations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we isolate the effect of truncation by studying an idealized error-free regime in which score estimation and discretization errors are ignored. This setting shows that collapse does not require imperfect learning: truncation of the reverse SDE (at <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) alone is sufficient to drive the recursive dynamics toward a nontrivial limiting distribution.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Time reversal and one-step dynamics</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the error-free regime, the transition from one generation to the next is defined by the true reverse SDE (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Reverse-time generation and truncation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) whose marginals, by time-reversal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anderson (1982)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haussmann and Pardoux (1986)</a></cite>, exactly match those of the forward SDE (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Forward diffusion ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). For each <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the model <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> can therefore be expressed using the OU operator (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Forward diffusion ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) applied at the truncation time <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{p}^{i+1}=\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(q_{i})=\mathrm{Law}(e^{-t_{0}/2}\mathbf{X}_{0}^{i}\,+\,\sqrt{1-e^{-t_{0}}}\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Law</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.392em">+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{i+1}=\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(q_{i})=\mathrm{Law}(e^{-t_{0}/2}\mathbf{X}_{0}^{i}\,+\,\sqrt{1-e^{-t_{0}}}\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus truncation alone introduces a mismatch between <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the target distribution at generation <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and it corresponds to a small amount of residual Gaussian noise. Since this smoothing is repeated recursively, Gaussian-convolved residuals accumulate over generations, and truncation alone leads to progressive drift away from the true data distribution <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Collapse distribution</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{i+1}=\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(\alpha p_{\mathrm{data}}+(1-\alpha)\hat{p}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{i+1}=\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(\alpha p_{\mathrm{data}}+(1-\alpha)\hat{p}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, the transition from the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th to the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math>-th generation is generation-independent. Therefore, the limiting behavior of the recursion amounts to the fixed-point problem of</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\mapsto\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}\bigl(\alpha p_{\mathrm{data}}+(1-\alpha)\mu\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\mapsto\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}\bigl(\alpha p_{\mathrm{data}}+(1-\alpha)\mu\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The following theorem shows that this recursion admits a unique fixed point and expresses it as a Neumann series (proof in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix D Proofs ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Collapse distribution: existence, uniqueness, and explicit form)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under <a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="Item A1 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>–<a href="#S2.I1.i3" title="Item A3 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a>, there exists a unique collapse distribution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}\in\mathcal{P}_{2}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> that is a fixed point of (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). It is given by,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}\;=\;\alpha\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}(1-\alpha)^{k}\,\mathcal{U}_{(k+1)t_{0}}(p_{\mathrm{data}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}\;=\;\alpha\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}(1-\alpha)^{k}\,\mathcal{U}_{(k+1)t_{0}}(p_{\mathrm{data}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, in the absence of score estimation and discretization errors, the sequence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{p}^{N})_{N\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{p}^{N})_{N\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> converges geometrically fast to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\infty}^{\star})\;\leq\;\kappa^{N}\,\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\mathrm{data}},p_{\infty}^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\infty}^{\star})\;\leq\;\kappa^{N}\,\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\mathrm{data}},p_{\infty}^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\kappa=\sqrt{1-\alpha}\,e^{-t_{0}/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msqrt><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=\sqrt{1-\alpha}\,e^{-t_{0}/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Collapse distribution: existence, uniqueness, and explicit form). ‣ Collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> shows that the collapse distribution is a geometric mixture of increasingly smoothed copies of the true data distribution. The <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>th component of the mixture, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{(k+1)t_{0}}(p_{\mathrm{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{(k+1)t_{0}}(p_{\mathrm{data}})</annotation></semantics></math>, is the data distribution smoothed by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> units of OU evolution, and it is weighted by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(1-\alpha)^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(1-\alpha)^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The rate of convergence depends on both the fresh-data proportion <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and the truncation time <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The larger the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the larger the Gaussian noise injection at each generation, and the faster the convergence; the larger the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the more fresh data injected in each generation, and the slower the convergence. The following result further quantifies the dependence of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> on the parameters <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in various limiting regimes (proof in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Proof of Corollary ‣ Appendix D Proofs ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Limiting behaviors)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The exact collapse distribution (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Theorem 3.1 (Collapse distribution: existence, uniqueness, and explicit form). ‣ Collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) satisfies the following:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">: </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\infty}^{\star},\;\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(p_{\mathrm{data}}))\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\infty}^{\star},\;\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(p_{\mathrm{data}}))\to 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to 0^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to 0^{+}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">: </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\infty}^{\star},\;\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{d}))\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\infty}^{\star},\;\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{d}))\to 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}\to 0^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}\to 0^{+}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">: </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\infty}^{\star},\;p_{\mathrm{data}})\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\infty}^{\star},\;p_{\mathrm{data}})\to 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iv)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}\not\equiv\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>≢</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}\not\equiv\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{d})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">:
</span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\infty}^{\star},\;p_{\mathrm{data}})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\infty}^{\star},\;p_{\mathrm{data}})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These limits have simple interpretations. When <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the recursion effectively disappears and only the one-step truncation bias remains. When <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the fraction of fresh data injected in each retraining generation vanishes, letting Gaussian smoothing compound without attenuation; this ultimately drives the recursion towards the standard Gaussian (the invariant measure of the OU semigroup). When <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}\to 0^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}\to 0^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, truncation vanishes and the collapse distribution approaches <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Collapse distribution: existence, uniqueness, and explicit form). ‣ Collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> gives a closed-form description of the collapse distribution, it does not directly identify how different features of the data distribution are affected by recursive training. In particular, the representation (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Theorem 3.1 (Collapse distribution: existence, uniqueness, and explicit form). ‣ Collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) does not
distinguish between low-order structure (e.g. mean, covariance) and finer non-Gaussian features (e.g. tails, oscillations, multimodality). We now refine this description by studying collapse mode by mode. Specifically, we analyze <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> in the Hermite polynomial basis, the spectral coordinates naturally associated with the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck dynamics. The functional analytic details for this section are collected in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Extended Background and Preliminary Results ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of square-integrable functions with respect to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. The inner product <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math> on this space is defined as
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle f,g\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}=\int f(\mathbf{x})g(\mathbf{x})\gamma(\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f,g\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}=\int f(\mathbf{x})g(\mathbf{x})\gamma(\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decomposition of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in Hermite Polynomial Basis</span>
For any multi-index <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}=(n_{1},\ldots,n_{d})\in\mathbb{N}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}=(n_{1},\ldots,n_{d})\in\mathbb{N}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the multivariate Hermite polynomial <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bogachev (2015)</a></cite> <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbf{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbf{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as
<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\mathbf{n}}(\mathbf{x}):=\prod_{j=1}^{d}H_{n_{j}}(x_{j})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\mathbf{n}}(\mathbf{x}):=\prod_{j=1}^{d}H_{n_{j}}(x_{j}),</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th univariate Hermite polynomial (defined in (<a href="#A2.E25" title="Equation 25 ‣ Hermite polynomials. ‣ B.2 Role of the Gaussian distribution and Decomposition in the Hermite Polynomial Basis ‣ Appendix B Extended Background and Preliminary Results ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>)). The family <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{H_{\mathbf{n}}\}_{\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{N}^{d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{H_{\mathbf{n}}\}_{\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{N}^{d}}</annotation></semantics></math> forms an orthogonal basis of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, any <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in L^{2}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in L^{2}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bogachev (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Janson (1997)</a></cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=\sum_{\mathbf{n}}\langle g,H_{\mathbf{n}}\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}H_{\mathbf{n}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>𝐧</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=\sum_{\mathbf{n}}\langle g,H_{\mathbf{n}}\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}H_{\mathbf{n}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now, for any probability measure <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\ll\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\ll\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\mathrm{d}\mu/\mathrm{d}\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\mathrm{d}\mu/\mathrm{d}\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be its Radon-Nikodym derivative (density ratio w.r.t. <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>), assumed to be in <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, writing <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\sum_{\mathbf{n}}a_{\mathbf{n}}H_{\mathbf{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\sum_{\mathbf{n}}a_{\mathbf{n}}H_{\mathbf{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have (Proposition <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition B.4. ‣ Hermite polynomials. ‣ B.2 Role of the Gaussian distribution and Decomposition in the Hermite Polynomial Basis ‣ Appendix B Extended Background and Preliminary Results ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}(\mathcal{U}_{t}\mu)}{\mathrm{d}\gamma}=\sum_{\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{N}^{d}}e^{-|\mathbf{n}|t/2}a_{\mathbf{n}}H_{\mathbf{n}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}(\mathcal{U}_{t}\mu)}{\mathrm{d}\gamma}=\sum_{\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{N}^{d}}e^{-|\mathbf{n}|t/2}a_{\mathbf{n}}H_{\mathbf{n}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">That is, OU smoothing attenuates higher-degree Hermite modes of the density ratio more strongly than lower-degree ones. Applying the decomposition (<a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) to the representation (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Theorem 3.1 (Collapse distribution: existence, uniqueness, and explicit form). ‣ Collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> requires that (i) <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}\ll\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}\ll\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, which is guaranteed by <a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="Item A1 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>–<a href="#S2.I1.i3" title="Item A3 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a>, and (ii) <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}p_{\mathrm{data}}}{\mathrm{d}\gamma}\in L^{2}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}p_{\mathrm{data}}}{\mathrm{d}\gamma}\in L^{2}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, which we assume.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I2.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A4)</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The data distribution <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> is such that <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathrm{data}}:=\frac{\mathrm{d}p_{\mathrm{data}}}{\mathrm{d}\gamma}\in L^{2}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathrm{data}}:=\frac{\mathrm{d}p_{\mathrm{data}}}{\mathrm{d}\gamma}\in L^{2}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#S3.I2.i4" title="Item A4 ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a> excludes distributions <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> with heavy tails relative to a Gaussian. However, our experiments on CIFAR-10 (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Self-regularization regime ‣ 4 Mitigating Model Collapse with Annealed Truncation Schedules ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) suggest that the spectral representation results in this section hold more widely in practice. Under <a href="#S3.I2.i4" title="Item A4 ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is also absolutely continuous with respect to <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, with density denoted by
<math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\infty}^{\star}:=\frac{\mathrm{d}p_{\infty}^{\star}}{\mathrm{d}\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\infty}^{\star}:=\frac{\mathrm{d}p_{\infty}^{\star}}{\mathrm{d}\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following result shows that the collapse dynamics act diagonally in the Hermite basis, with an explicit mode-wise attenuation factor (proof in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS4" title="D.4 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix D Proofs ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Spectral representation of the collapse distribution)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="Item A1 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>, <a href="#S2.I1.i2" title="Item A2 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A2</span></a>, <a href="#S2.I1.i3" title="Item A3 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.I2.i4" title="Item A4 ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a>, the density <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\infty}^{\star}=\frac{\mathrm{d}p_{\infty}^{\star}}{\mathrm{d}\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\infty}^{\star}=\frac{\mathrm{d}p_{\infty}^{\star}}{\mathrm{d}\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> of the error-free collapse distribution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> admits the expansion</span></p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\infty}^{\star}=\sum_{\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{N}^{d}}m_{\mathbf{n}}(\alpha,t_{0})\,\big\langle f_{\mathrm{data}},H_{\mathbf{n}}\big\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}\,H_{\mathbf{n}},\qquad m_{\mathbf{n}}(\alpha,t_{0})=\frac{\alpha\,e^{-|\mathbf{n}|t_{0}/2}}{1-(1-\alpha)e^{-|\mathbf{n}|t_{0}/2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\infty}^{\star}=\sum_{\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{N}^{d}}m_{\mathbf{n}}(\alpha,t_{0})\,\big\langle f_{\mathrm{data}},H_{\mathbf{n}}\big\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}\,H_{\mathbf{n}},\qquad m_{\mathbf{n}}(\alpha,t_{0})=\frac{\alpha\,e^{-|\mathbf{n}|t_{0}/2}}{1-(1-\alpha)e^{-|\mathbf{n}|t_{0}/2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently, for every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{N}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{N}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big\langle f_{\infty}^{\star},H_{\mathbf{n}}\big\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}=m_{\mathbf{n}}(\alpha,t_{0})\,\big\langle f_{\mathrm{data}},H_{\mathbf{n}}\big\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\langle f_{\infty}^{\star},H_{\mathbf{n}}\big\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}=m_{\mathbf{n}}(\alpha,t_{0})\,\big\langle f_{\mathrm{data}},H_{\mathbf{n}}\big\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Equation (<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ Proposition 3.3 (Spectral representation of the collapse distribution). ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) shows that the collapse distribution is obtained from <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> by a mode-wise attenuation in the Hermite basis relative to the Gaussian reference measure <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. The attenuation factor <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{\mathbf{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{\mathbf{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> is monotone decreasing in the total degree <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐧</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that high-order polynomials, which encode increasingly fine non-Gaussian features such as tails, oscillations, and multimodality, are suppressed more strongly than low-order ones, which encode Gaussian-like structure of a distribution relative to <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. This observation matches previous results in which model collapse has, for example, been associated with a degradation of tails <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shumailov et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alemohammad et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shumailov et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Moreover, the more fresh-data is injected at each retraining generation, the fewer modes of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> are affected by the recursion, consistent with similar observations in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bertrand et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gerstgrasser et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khelifa et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3 (Spectral representation of the collapse distribution). ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> thus completes Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Collapse distribution: existence, uniqueness, and explicit form). ‣ Collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> by quantifying the mode-wise effect of <span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">truncation</span> on <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the attenuation of high-degree Hermite modes of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> on a two-dimensional distribution for varying <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and how this evolves with the proportion of fresh data.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13796v1/hermite_pdata_and_theory_limits_row.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/134;" width="476" height="134" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Hermite-mode attenuation of <math id="S3.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> for varying <math id="S3.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="S3.F1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Each heatmap displays the coefficient <math id="S3.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\langle f,H_{n_{1},n_{2}}\right\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\langle f,H_{n_{1},n_{2}}\right\rangle_{L^{2}(\gamma)}</annotation></semantics></math> of the density ratio <math id="S3.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\mathrm{d}p/\mathrm{d}\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\mathrm{d}p/\mathrm{d}\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> in the two-dimensional Hermite basis, with <math id="S3.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the polynomial degrees along the two coordinates.
<span id="S3.F1.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Right</span>: decomposition of <math id="S3.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, chosen as an oscillatory distribution with substantial energy in medium- and high-degree Hermite modes. <span id="S3.F1.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remaining panels</span>: similar spectral decomposition of the fixed-point distribution <math id="S3.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{0.1,0.5,0.9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{0.1,0.5,0.9\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As predicted by Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3 (Spectral representation of the collapse distribution). ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, the attenuation is stronger for smaller <math id="S3.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Mitigating Model Collapse with Annealed Truncation Schedules</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior studies of model collapse for recursively trained general generative models identified the fresh-data proportion <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> as a key parameter to stabilize or attenuate recursive degradation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bertrand et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gerstgrasser et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khelifa et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, for diffusion models, Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Collapse distribution: existence, uniqueness, and explicit form). ‣ Collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3 (Spectral representation of the collapse distribution). ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> show that for any fresh-data proportion <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a smoothing bias created by the fixed truncation time <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\infty}^{\star},\;p_{\mathrm{data}})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\infty}^{\star},\;p_{\mathrm{data}})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#A6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ F.1 Synthetic 2D Gaussian mixtures ‣ Appendix F Experiments ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Experiments ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>) illustrates the effect of <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on collapse when varying <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> on CIFAR-10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krizhevsky (2009)</a></cite>, showing that while a higher fresh-data injection rate slows down collapse, it does not prevent it altogether. This suggests a complementary intervention on truncation: modifying the sampling operator across generations by progressively decreasing the truncation times. The resulting annealed schedule targets the specific source of bias identified above, namely the residual OU smoothing due to early stopping.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.13796v1/14_annealed_truncation.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/142;" width="476" height="142"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Annealed truncation schedules eliminate recursive compounding (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Asymptotic effect of generation-dependent truncation). ‣ Generation-dependent truncation ‣ 4 Mitigating Model Collapse with Annealed Truncation Schedules ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>).<span id="S4.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Error-free recursion on a 2D Gaussian mixture, <math id="S4.F2.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(\hat{p}^{N}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(\hat{p}^{N}\,\|\,p_{\mathrm{data}})</annotation></semantics></math> vs. generation <math id="S4.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> for different fresh-data proportions <math id="S4.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{0.1,0.5,0.9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{0.1,0.5,0.9\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each plot compares four schedules: fixed at <math id="S4.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> (red), decreasing and converging to <math id="S4.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\infty}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\infty}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> (purple), and annealed converging to <math id="S4.F2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S4.F2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}/(1+i)^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}/(1+i)^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.F2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> (orange) and <math id="S4.F2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=2</annotation></semantics></math> (blue). The annealed schedules drive KL toward <math id="S4.F2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> at rates increasing with <math id="S4.F2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, while the fixed and positive-limit schedules plateau at <math id="S4.F2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(p_{\infty}^{\star},p_{\mathrm{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(p_{\infty}^{\star},p_{\mathrm{data}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(p_{\infty}^{(t_{\infty})},p_{\mathrm{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(p_{\infty}^{(t_{\infty})},p_{\mathrm{data}})</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Experiment details in Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Experiments ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generation-dependent truncation</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a sequence of truncation times <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{0}^{(N)})_{N\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{0}^{(N)})_{N\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that, in the error-free regime, the recursion becomes</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{N+1}=\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}^{(N)}}\bigl(\alpha\,p_{\mathrm{data}}+(1-\alpha)\hat{p}^{N}\bigr),\qquad N\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{N+1}=\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}^{(N)}}\bigl(\alpha\,p_{\mathrm{data}}+(1-\alpha)\hat{p}^{N}\bigr),\qquad N\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The following result (proof in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS5" title="D.5 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix D Proofs ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.5</span></a>) gives an exact representation of the iterates <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by the adaptive recursion (<a href="#S4.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Generation-dependent truncation ‣ 4 Mitigating Model Collapse with Annealed Truncation Schedules ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), characterizes their limit when the schedule converges and shows that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be exactly recovered.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Asymptotic effect of generation-dependent truncation)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{0}^{(i)})_{i\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{0}^{(i)})_{i\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of positive truncation times, and consider
the error-free recursion (<a href="#S4.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Generation-dependent truncation ‣ 4 Mitigating Model Collapse with Annealed Truncation Schedules ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). Then, for every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{N}=\alpha\sum_{m=0}^{N-1}(1-\alpha)^{m}\,\mathcal{U}_{\sigma_{m,N}}(p_{\mathrm{data}})+(1-\alpha)^{N}\mathcal{U}_{s_{0,N}}(p_{\mathrm{data}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{N}=\alpha\sum_{m=0}^{N-1}(1-\alpha)^{m}\,\mathcal{U}_{\sigma_{m,N}}(p_{\mathrm{data}})+(1-\alpha)^{N}\mathcal{U}_{s_{0,N}}(p_{\mathrm{data}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{m,N}:=\sum_{\ell=N-1-m}^{N-1}t_{0}^{(\ell)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{m,N}:=\sum_{\ell=N-1-m}^{N-1}t_{0}^{(\ell)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0,N}:=\sum_{\ell=0}^{N-1}t_{0}^{(\ell)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0,N}:=\sum_{\ell=0}^{N-1}t_{0}^{(\ell)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}^{(N)}\to t_{\infty}\in[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}^{(N)}\to t_{\infty}\in[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{N}\,\xrightarrow[N\to\infty]{\mathcal{W}_{2}}\,p_{\infty}^{(t_{\infty})}:=\alpha\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}(1-\alpha)^{m}\,\mathcal{U}_{(m+1)t_{\infty}}p_{\mathrm{data}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mo lspace="0.448em">→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒲</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{N}\,\xrightarrow[N\to\infty]{\mathcal{W}_{2}}\,p_{\infty}^{(t_{\infty})}:=\alpha\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}(1-\alpha)^{m}\,\mathcal{U}_{(m+1)t_{\infty}}p_{\mathrm{data}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\infty}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\infty}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{(t_{\infty})}=p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{(t_{\infty})}=p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the recursive compounding effect disappears asymptotically.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Asymptotic effect of generation-dependent truncation). ‣ Generation-dependent truncation ‣ 4 Mitigating Model Collapse with Annealed Truncation Schedules ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> shows that the long-run effect of a varying truncation schedule is controlled by its <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">asymptotic level</em> <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>: because fresh data are re-injected at every generation, recent truncation times dominate the long-run dynamics. If the schedule converges to a positive limit, collapse persists as in the fixed-truncation regime with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=t_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=t_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, but with smaller residual noise if the sequence <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t^{(i)}_{0})_{i\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t^{(i)}_{0})_{i\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is decreasing. By contrast, if <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}^{(i)}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}^{(i)}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
then the error-free recursion converges back to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>; asymptotically, no
nontrivial collapse distribution remains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-annealed schedules</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the schedule <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}^{(i)}(\beta)=t_{0}/(1+i)^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}^{(i)}(\beta)=t_{0}/(1+i)^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, implying <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\mathrm{data}})\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\mathrm{data}})\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> in the error-free regime. However, different values of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> induce different <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finite-generation</em> distortions: larger <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> drives the truncation time to zero faster, therefore suppressing recursive attenuation more aggressively over a fixed number of generations. Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Mitigating Model Collapse with Annealed Truncation Schedules ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares four schedules over <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> generations at <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{0.1,0.5,0.9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{0.1,0.5,0.9\}</annotation></semantics></math>: fixed at <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, decreasing to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\infty}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\infty}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-annealed schedules <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}/(1+i)^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}/(1+i)^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The fixed and shifted schedules plateau at the predicted floors <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(p_{\infty}^{\star},p_{\mathrm{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(p_{\infty}^{\star},p_{\mathrm{data}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{KL}(p_{\infty}^{(t_{\infty})},p_{\mathrm{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>KL</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{KL}(p_{\infty}^{(t_{\infty})},p_{\mathrm{data}})</annotation></semantics></math>, while the annealed schedules drive KL toward <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> at rates increasing with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming that recursive compounding is eliminated whenever <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\infty}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\infty}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Convergence slows with smaller <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, as expected from <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\kappa=\sqrt{1-\alpha}\,e^{-t_{0}/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msqrt><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=\sqrt{1-\alpha}\,e^{-t_{0}/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Self-regularization regime</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although taking <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}^{(i)}(\beta)\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}^{(i)}(\beta)\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> grows raises the question of score estimation stability, in some cases the recursive structure (<a href="#S1.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Setting ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) provides a self-regularization mechanism: the synthetic component of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has already accumulated cumulative smoothing, which keeps its Fisher information bounded even as <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}^{(i)}(\beta)\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}^{(i)}(\beta)\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. We make this precise in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Self-Regularization and Annealed Truncation ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2606.13796v1/fig_fid_to_spinf_gen10_shifted.svg" id="S4.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/267;" width="476" height="267"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><math id="S4.F3.sf1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FID}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\infty}^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">FID</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋆</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FID}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\infty}^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> vs. generation <math id="S4.F3.sf1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2606.13796v1/fig_hf_deficit_heatmap_first10.png" id="S4.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/264;" width="476" height="264" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">High-frequency energy ratio <math id="S4.F3.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{HF}(\hat{p}^{N})/\mathrm{HF}(p_{\mathrm{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>HF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>HF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{HF}(\hat{p}^{N})/\mathrm{HF}(p_{\mathrm{data}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Empirical validation of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Geometric convergence and stability). ‣ Concentration around 𝑝_∞^⋆ ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 5.3 (Mode-dependent error propagation). ‣ Spectral Decomposition in the Learned Regime ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> (CIFAR-10).<span id="S4.F3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Recursive training for <math id="S4.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> generations at <math id="S4.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9\}</annotation></semantics></math>. </span>(a)<span id="S4.F3.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The iterates <math id="S4.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> contract toward <math id="S4.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> at a rate increasing with <math id="S4.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, matching the contraction factor <math id="S4.F3.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\kappa(\alpha)=\sqrt{1-\alpha}\,e^{-t_{0}/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msqrt><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(\alpha)=\sqrt{1-\alpha}\,e^{-t_{0}/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and settle at a common floor consistent with stability around <math id="S4.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Geometric convergence and stability). ‣ Concentration around 𝑝_∞^⋆ ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).
</span>(b)<span id="S4.F3.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> High-frequency deficit <math id="S4.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\mathrm{HF}(\hat{p}^{N})/\mathrm{HF}(p_{\mathrm{data}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>HF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>HF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\mathrm{HF}(\hat{p}^{N})/\mathrm{HF}(p_{\mathrm{data}})</annotation></semantics></math> over recursive generations and fresh-data proportions <math id="S4.F3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Larger values indicate a stronger loss of high-frequency content relative to the data distribution. The deficit grows fastest when <math id="S4.F3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is small and <math id="S4.F3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases, validating the low-pass mechanism of Propositions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3 (Spectral representation of the collapse distribution). ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 5.3 (Mode-dependent error propagation). ‣ Spectral Decomposition in the Learned Regime ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> on real images. Together, the results show that <math id="S4.F3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts both the convergence dynamics and the spectral signature of the empirical limit. Details in Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Experiments ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The idealized analysis in the last two sections isolated the effect of truncation, but in practice, recursive training is also affected by score estimation and discretization errors. In this section we provide robustness results quantifying how perturbations of the learned sampling operator, induced by errors in score estimation and discretization, affect the divergence between <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and the ideal limit <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. We return to the setting of a fixed truncation time <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Score Estimation and Discretization Errors</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing the ideal and reverse SDEs (<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Reverse-time generation and truncation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Reverse-time generation and truncation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), the score estimation error at generation <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{i}(\mathbf{x},s)=\mathbf{s}_{\theta_{i}}(\mathbf{x},s)-\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log q_{i,s}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{i}(\mathbf{x},s)=\mathbf{s}_{\theta_{i}}(\mathbf{x},s)-\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log q_{i,s}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. For a given score network, <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> depends, through the target <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, on the mixing factor <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the ambient dimension <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th generation training sample size <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The learned reverse SDE (<a href="#S2.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Reverse-time generation and truncation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is further discretized via a numerical scheme on a partition <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{t_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{t_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{0},T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{0},T]</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th generation <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">one-step sampling error</span> <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the combined effect of score estimation and discretization. We define it as the <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance between the output of the imperfect sampler <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{S}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{S}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and that of the ideal sampler <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}\equiv\delta_{i}(d,n_{i},\{t_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K_{i}},\alpha)\;=\;\mathcal{W}_{2}\!\bigl(\hat{\mathcal{S}}_{i}(q_{i}),\;\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(q_{i})\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}\equiv\delta_{i}(d,n_{i},\{t_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K_{i}},\alpha)\;=\;\mathcal{W}_{2}\!\bigl(\hat{\mathcal{S}}_{i}(q_{i}),\;\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(q_{i})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Concrete instantiations of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several works make the dependence between <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,n_{i},\{t_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K_{i}},\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,n_{i},\{t_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K_{i}},\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> explicit via upper bounds. For the Euler–Maruyama discretization, Arsenyan et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arsenyan et al. (2025)</a></cite> establish optimal <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> bounds, and
for the exponential integrator, Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2023c)</a></cite> provide KL bounds that can be transferred to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> under moment assumptions. Higher-order schemes such as midpoint randomization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu and Yu (2025)</a></cite>, stochastic Runge–Kutta methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and accelerated DDIM-type samplers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> have been shown to have improved dependence on the step size or on the number of function evaluations. Deterministic samplers based on the probability flow ODE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao and Zhu (2025)</a></cite> admit analogous <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> guarantees under log-concavity. Our results are general and applicable to any of these schemes.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Concentration around <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math></h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now show that the imperfect iterates <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> track the ideal collapse distribution <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> at a geometric rate, perturbed by the one-step errors <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (proof in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS6" title="D.6 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix D Proofs ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Geometric convergence and stability)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="Item A1 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a></span>–<a href="#S2.I1.i3" title="Item A3 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then for each <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\infty}^{\star})\;\leq\;\kappa^{N}\,\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\mathrm{data}},p_{\infty}^{\star})\;+\;\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\kappa^{N-1-i}\,\delta_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.335em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\infty}^{\star})\;\leq\;\kappa^{N}\,\mathcal{W}_{2}(p_{\mathrm{data}},p_{\infty}^{\star})\;+\;\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\kappa^{N-1-i}\,\delta_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\kappa=\sqrt{(1-\alpha)}e^{-t_{0}/2}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=\sqrt{(1-\alpha)}e^{-t_{0}/2}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i\geq 0}\delta_{i}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i\geq 0}\delta_{i}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then
</span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\infty}^{\star})\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\infty}^{\star})\to 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{i}\delta_{i}&lt;\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{i}\delta_{i}&lt;\delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then
</span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\limsup_{N\to\infty}\mathcal{W}_{2}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\infty}^{\star})\leq\frac{\delta}{1-\kappa}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\limsup_{N\to\infty}\mathcal{W}_{2}(\hat{p}^{N},p_{\infty}^{\star})\leq\frac{\delta}{1-\kappa}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Geometric convergence and stability). ‣ Concentration around 𝑝_∞^⋆ ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> establishes that <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> remains within a <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-ball of radius <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta/(1-\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta/(1-\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, and Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Self-regularization regime ‣ 4 Mitigating Model Collapse with Annealed Truncation Schedules ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates this robustness to error in a real image dataset (CIFAR-10) across varying levels of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Proposition <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition D.2 (Linear perturbation recursion). ‣ D.7 Explicit characterization of perturbation ‣ Appendix D Proofs ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> (Appendix <a href="#A4.SS7" title="D.7 Explicit characterization of perturbation ‣ Appendix D Proofs ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.7</span></a>) complements Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Geometric convergence and stability). ‣ Concentration around 𝑝_∞^⋆ ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> by providing an explicit characterization of the perturbation <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{p}^{N}-p_{\infty}^{\star})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{p}^{N}-p_{\infty}^{\star})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Spectral Decomposition in the Learned Regime</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The spectral viewpoint on the error-free collapse distribution <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> can be extended to the general setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following result (proof in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS8" title="D.8 Proof of Proposition ‣ Appendix D Proofs ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.8</span></a>) shows that the error ball around <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> identified in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Geometric convergence and stability). ‣ Concentration around 𝑝_∞^⋆ ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> is spectrally shaped: high-frequency modes (<math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbf{n}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbf{n}|</annotation></semantics></math> large) contract with geometric ratio <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\mathbf{n}}\ll\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\mathbf{n}}\ll\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, so errors in fine-scale structure are suppressed exponentially faster than errors in coarse structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Mode-dependent error propagation)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under <a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="Item A1 ‣ Regularity conditions ‣ 2 Background ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A1</span></a>–<a href="#S3.I2.i4" title="Item A4 ‣ 3.1 Spectral decomposition of the collapse distribution ‣ 3 Limiting Collapse Distribution in the Error-free Regime ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a>, further assume that for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the learned distribution in the imperfect regime satisfies <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\hat{p}^{i}/\mathrm{d}\gamma\in L^{2}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\hat{p}^{i}/\mathrm{d}\gamma\in L^{2}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}(\hat{p}^{N}-p_{\infty}^{\star})/\mathrm{d}\gamma\in L^{2}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}(\hat{p}^{N}-p_{\infty}^{\star})/\mathrm{d}\gamma\in L^{2}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> and, decomposing mode by mode, for each multi-index <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{N}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}\in\mathbb{N}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\hat{p}^{N}-p_{\infty}^{\star},\,H_{\mathbf{n}}\rangle\;=\;\kappa_{\mathbf{n}}^{N}\,\langle p_{\mathrm{data}}-p_{\infty}^{\star},\,H_{\mathbf{n}}\rangle\;+\;\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\kappa_{\mathbf{n}}^{N-1-i}\,\langle\hat{p}^{i+1}-\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(q_{i}),\,H_{\mathbf{n}}\rangle," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>𝐧</mi><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.335em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>κ</mi><mi>𝐧</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\hat{p}^{N}-p_{\infty}^{\star},\,H_{\mathbf{n}}\rangle\;=\;\kappa_{\mathbf{n}}^{N}\,\langle p_{\mathrm{data}}-p_{\infty}^{\star},\,H_{\mathbf{n}}\rangle\;+\;\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}\kappa_{\mathbf{n}}^{N-1-i}\,\langle\hat{p}^{i+1}-\mathcal{U}_{t_{0}}(q_{i}),\,H_{\mathbf{n}}\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\mathbf{n}}:=(1-\alpha)\,e^{-|\mathbf{n}|t_{0}/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\mathbf{n}}:=(1-\alpha)\,e^{-|\mathbf{n}|t_{0}/2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mode-dependent contraction rate.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Self-correction of high-frequency errors)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 5.3 (Mode-dependent error propagation). ‣ Spectral Decomposition in the Learned Regime ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> refines the scalar <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> bound of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Geometric convergence and stability). ‣ Concentration around 𝑝_∞^⋆ ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>. It indicates that the effective error ball is an ellipsoid whose semi-axes <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta/(1-\kappa_{\mathbf{n}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>𝐧</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta/(1-\kappa_{\mathbf{n}})</annotation></semantics></math> shrink rapidly with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbf{n}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐧</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbf{n}|</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than a ball of radius <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta/(1-\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta/(1-\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math> as suggested by Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Geometric convergence and stability). ‣ Concentration around 𝑝_∞^⋆ ‣ 5 Robustness of Limiting Distribution in the Presence of Errors ‣ Recursively Trained Diffusion Models: Limiting Collapse Distribution and Spectral Characterization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work provides an asymptotic characterization of model collapse in recursively trained diffusion models. First, in an idealized regime without discretization and score estimation errors, we show that collapse still occurs because of the truncation of the reverse diffusion near <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which is standard in practical sampling. Truncation induces a residual Gaussian smoothing in each generation, which compounds and drives the recursive dynamics to a unique limiting distribution <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, a geometric mixture of progressively smoothed copies of <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The spectral representation of <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\infty}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\infty}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> further shows that this limiting distribution corresponds to a mode-wise attenuation of the data distribution in the Hermite basis, making precise the sense in which recursive diffusion training acts as a low-pass filter.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our results also highlight that in diffusion models, model collapse is not solely driven by the proportion of fresh-data <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Increasing <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> attenuates recursive degradation, but it does not eliminate the smoothing effect induced by a fixed truncation time <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. This observation motivates a diffusion-specific route to mitigating collapse, complementary to fresh-data injection: by decreasing the truncation time across recursive generations (annealed truncation schedules), one can eliminate the asymptotic compounding effect in the error-free regime.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Future directions and limitations</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several directions remain open. First, our analysis treats score estimation and discretization errors through abstract one-step perturbations. A natural next step is to combine the present fixed-point analysis with finite-sample score-learning bounds, thereby obtaining explicit end-to-end guarantees in terms of model class, sample size, dimension, and numerical solver. Second, the annealed truncation schedules studied here are prescribed, rather than learned or adapted to the current generation. Designing practical adaptive rules that balance numerical stability against recursive bias is an important direction. Third, the spectral characterization is developed for the variance-preserving OU diffusion and the Hermite basis associated with its invariant Gaussian measure. Extending this perspective to other diffusion parameterizations, alternative noising processes, or non-Gaussian reference measures may reveal more general spectral mechanisms underlying collapse.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgements</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">NBK is supported by a G-Research Trinity College Studentship, and RET is supported by the EPSRC Probabilistic AI Hub (EP/Y028783/1).</p>
</div>
</section>
